Based on the memory context, here are two simple, dependency-free caching systems for your app's API responses.

### 1. In-Memory Cache with Time-To-Live (TTL)

This approach is fast and straightforward but does not persist data across page refreshes or browser sessions. It uses a JavaScript class to manage cached items and their expiration.

**Implementation:**
```javascript
class WeatherCache {
  // Default TTL is 1 hour, but can be configured.
  constructor(ttl = 3600000) { 
    this.cache = {};
    this.ttl = ttl;
  }

  get(city) {
    if (this.cache[city] && this.cache[city].expires > Date.now()) {
      return this.cache[city].data;
    }
    return null;
  }

  set(city, data) {
    this.cache[city] = {
      data,
      expires: Date.now() + this.ttl
    };
  }
}

const cache = new WeatherCache(); // Or new WeatherCache(300000) for 5 minutes.

async function fetchWeather(city) {
  const cachedResponse = cache.get(city);
  if (cachedResponse) {
    console.log('Returning cached data for', city);
    return cachedResponse;
  }

  // Fetch data from the API if not in cache
  const response = await fetch(`https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${city}&units=metric&appid=${process.env.OPENWEATHER_API_KEY}`);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
  }
  const jsonData = await response.json();
  cache.set(city, jsonData);
  return jsonData;
}
```
